  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Noticeably cooler/pleasant and rain-free start to\nthe week \n\nSky has cleared out. But, thin high clouds are moving into the\nwest, and will gradually overspread most of Central PA\novernight. That shouldn't matter to the river valley fog which\nshould be starting to show up by midnight. \n\nPrev...\n\nRefreshing airmass behind cold front today with dewpoints in the\n40s and 50s. Fair weather cu across much of the region with max\ncoverage near the Great Lakes. Max temps will be 10 to 15 \ndegrees lower/cooler vs. Sunday ranging from the mid 60s in the \nnorthern tier mtns to the mid 70s in the southern valleys along \nthe MD line. \n\nStrong radiational cooling pattern tonight into early Tuesday \nmorning with very low pwats beneath area of high pressure \nmigrating over CPA. Min temps into the upper 30s are definitely\nin play in the usual northern tier cold spots with 40-50F lows \nanticipated elsewhere (4-8F below climo). This setup favors \nvalley fog formation with localized visibility restrictions \nbelow 1 mile. \n\nWide 25-35 degree diurnal range fcst Tuesday with another \npleasant afternoon with max temps in the upper 60s to mid 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Warm/humid conditions return mid to late week \nwith best odds for rain Thursday \n\nReturn SSW flow on the back side of the high pressure system \nwill pump the dewpoints and moisture/humidity back up from mid \nto late week. Best rain/QPF signal at this time is on Thursday\n9/17 as a weak front sinks southward from the eastern Great \nLakes. If the front can push south of the MD line, most of \nFriday could be dry. Precip odds then steadily increase over the\nweekend ahead of next frontal system progression from the Great\nLakes. Large scale upper level ridging should support a mid to \nlate week warming trend with the largest hi/lo departure from \nclimo Wed-Thu.",
